    "caveats": [
      "All monetary fields (invoice.Total, invoiceline.UnitPrice, and track.UnitPrice) are already denominated in USD; do not apply a cents-to-dollars conversion.",
      "Use invoice.Total for invoice-level revenue. Joining invoice to invoiceline creates multiple rows per invoice, so SUM(invoice.Total) after that join will overstate revenue.",
      "For product-level sales analysis, use SUM(invoiceline.UnitPrice * invoiceline.Quantity) after joining invoiceline to track and catalog dimensions. This is the transaction price, whereas track.UnitPrice is the current catalog/list price.",
      "Invoice lines have quantity 1 in the supplied data, but calculations should still multiply UnitPrice by Quantity.",
      "InvoiceDate spans 2009-01-01 through 2013-12-22. Use invoice.InvoiceDate for sales timing, not customer attributes or catalog dates.",
      "Customer geography is account-profile data, while invoice billing geography is the address recorded at the time of billing; use BillingCountry/BillingCity for historical sales geography.",
      "playlisttrack is a many-to-many bridge. Joining it to sales can duplicate invoice lines when a track belongs to multiple playlists; aggregate sales before joining, or restrict to a single playlist.",
      "employee.ReportsTo is a nullable self-reference: the top-level employee has no manager. customer.SupportRepId identifies the assigned employee.",
      "Company, customer State, invoice BillingState, composer, and several contact fields can be null; account for nulls explicitly in grouping and filtering.",
      "Track.AlbumId, GenreId, and MediaTypeId are catalog relationships. Album rolls up to artist; avoid mixing catalog track counts with playlist membership counts without DISTINCT track identifiers."
